            <ArticleTitle>The gold(iii)-CO bond: a missing piece in the gold carbonyl complex landscape.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1603-1606</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1039/c6cc09879g</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We investigate the Au(iii)-CO bond in the [(C∧N∧C)Au(iii)CO]+ complex via charge-displacement (CD) analysis, revealing surprising peculiarities with respect to the Au(i)-CO bond. It shows a large Au(iii) ← CO σ donation and, unexpectedly, an even larger Au(iii) → CO π back-donation. The overall bonding picture, including a marked anisotropy in the Au(iii) → CO in-plane and out-of-plane π back-donation components, is consistent with a CO carbon atom highly susceptible to nucleophilic attack.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
